  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nRain continues to exit east this afternoon, with just a few \nlingering showers east of I-35. The associated clouds and rain-\ncooled air will help keep temperatures in the 80s today, with the \nwarmest temperatures where skies have already cleared across north-\ncentral KS. As WAA strengthens this evening, still could see a brief \nshower or storm pop-up across far eastern KS. This has been hinted \nat with some recent CAM guidance, and seems plausible given the \nmoist airmass. A veering wind profile leading to 30 kts of effective \nshear could support some brief storm organization, and perhaps some \nvery localized gusty winds. But with subsidence and poor mid-level \nlapse rates, the most likely scenario is for little more than a \nrumble of thunder.\n\nAttention for the rest of the forecast period turns to heat. An \nupper high quickly builds over the High Plains tonight into \ntomorrow, with 500 mb heights approaching 600 dam. It then stays \nparked across the region into early next week. This is a classic \npattern for hazardous heat and humidity. As usual, guidance has \ndifferent depictions of how deeply we mix. But given the recent \nrainfall and current mid/upper 70s dewpoints across eastern OK, \nthere is much more confidence in the more humid and less deeply \nmixed guidance. (Can almost immediately discount the GFS and its \ntypical very hot/dry overmixing bias.) This would support high \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 90s Saturday and Sunday, with \ndewpoints in the mid to upper 70s. Resulting heat indices should \nreach or exceed 105 area-wide. Have thus gone with a Heat Advisory \nfor most of the area, with a small Extreme Heat Warning over east-\ncentral KS where confidence in 110+ heat indices is greatest. This \nwarning may need to be extended northwestward.\n\nKept the Extreme Heat Watch in place for Monday and Tuesday, as \nconfidence in the hazardous heat gradually decreases with time. \nMonday in all likelihood will again see heat indices 105-113, but \nthere are a few hints in guidance that convection could develop over \neastern Nebraska Sunday night and keep some clouds around into the \nday Monday. So will refrain from going with a heat warning until \nthere's better confidence that won't happen. By Tuesday, more \nguidance pushes a boundary southward into the area as a shortwave \ndrops southeast over the Great Lakes. Clouds and lingering showers \ncould also be present with this. So confidence is even lower with \nheat indices 105+ Tuesday through Thursday, especially with \nnortheastern extent across the area. But even with a slight \ncooldown, conditions should stay hot as the upper ridge never gets \ntoo far to our west.",
